  "summary_en": "Opinion of the German Ethics Council on the ethical use of robotics in elderly and disability care. It analyses micro, meso and macro levels of responsibility, insists robots complement rather than replace human care, and recommends high safety standards, strict liability, participatory design, transparent responsibility structures, evidence before reimbursement and staff training.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Robotics must complement, not substitute, interpersonal care and serve the wellbeing and self-determination of care recipients.",
   "Robots need high safety standards; strict liability, burden-of-proof rules and insurance should compensate damage; lawmakers should review medical device and liability law.",
   "Transparent responsibility structures are needed to prevent erosion of responsibility.",
   "Evidence of improved care quality required before inclusion in standard insurance-funded care.",
   "Care curricula and continuing education should include new technologies and their ethics; funding must not cut staffing."
  ],
